<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>More Practice with Congruence and Divides</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            It will be important to both internalize the exact definitions, and
            have some intuition.
            This practice is to help with that!
        </p>

        <activity>
            <title>Defintions</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    First, let's start with definitions!
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        State the precise definition of divides below.
                        That is, complete the sentence a
                        <fillin characters="20"/> integer <m>a</m> divides an
                        integer <m>b</m> if
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        A nonzero integer <m>a</m> divides an integer <m>b</m>,
                        written <m>a\mid b</m>, if there exists
                        <m>k\in\mathbb{Z}</m> such that <m>b=mk</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        State the precise definition congruence below.
                        That is, complete the sentence for
                        <m>a,b \in <fillin characters="5"/></m> and
                        <m>m \in <fillin characters="5"/></m>, we say <m>a</m>
                        is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo <m>m</m> or (in
                        notation) <fillin characters="20"/> if
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        For <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>m\in\mathbb{N}</m>,
                        we say <m>a</m> is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo
                        <m>m</m>, written <m>a \equiv b \pmod{m}</m> provided
                        that <m>m \mid a-b</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <activity workspace=".3in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Now let's practice doing the math!
                    You can do it intuitively if you've got an idea, but then
                    check with the official defintion!
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine if the following are true or false.
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m> 3\mid 12 </m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>12 \mid 3</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>7 \mid 50</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>5 \mid 0</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>0 \mid 5</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>-4 \mid 12</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>6\mid -18</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>1 \mid 9999999</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m> 3\mid 12 </m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    True.
                                    We know <m>3\mid 12</m> because
                                    <m>12 = 3(4)</m> and <m>4\in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>12 \mid 3</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    False.
                                    We know <m>12 \nmid 3</m> as there is no
                                    integer <m>k</m> such that <m>3 = 12(k)</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>7 \mid 50</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    False.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>5 \mid 0</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    True.
                                    We know <m>5\mid 0</m> as <m>0 = 5(0)</m>
                                    and <m>0\in \mathbb{Z}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>0 \mid 5</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    False.
                                    <m>0\nmid 5</m>, as the definition of
                                    divides prohibits the divisor from being
                                    <m>0. </m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>-4 \mid 12</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>6\mid -18</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>1 \mid 9999999</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For each of the following, determine if true or false.
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>17 \equiv  5 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>23 \equiv  2 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>8 \equiv  -4 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>100 \equiv  1 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>-1 \equiv 6 \pmod{7}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>45 \equiv 0 \pmod{9}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>17 \equiv  5 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    True. We know <m>17\equiv  5 \pmod{6}</m>
                                    because <m>6(2) \mid 17-5</m> and <m>2\in \mathbb{Z}</m>
                                    so <m>6\mid (17-5)</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>23 \equiv  2 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    False. We know  <m>23 \not\equiv  2 \pmod{6}</m> because
                                    <m>6\nmid (23-2)</m> as there is no integer <m>k</m> such
                                    that <m>23-2 = 6k</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>8 \equiv  -4 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>100 \equiv  1 \pmod{6}</m>
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    False.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>-1 \equiv 6 \pmod{7}</m>
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>45 \equiv 0 \pmod{9}</m>
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    True.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <activity workspace=".5in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    More advanced practice.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>n</m> such that <m>n\mid 15</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The integers are <m>\pm 1, \pm 3, \pm 5, </m> and <m>\pm 15</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>n</m> with <m>0\le n\le 100</m>
                        such that <m>11\mid n</m>.
                    </p>
                  
                </statement>
                   <solution>
                    <p>
                        The integers are <m>0, 11, 22,33, 44,55,66,77,88,</m> and <m>99</m>. Don't
                        forget about <m>0</m>!
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the smallest nonnegative integer <m>x</m> such that
                        <m>47\equiv x\pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The smallest nonnegative integer such that <m>47\equiv x\pmod{5}</m>
                        is <m>x=2</m>. Note that this is the remainder when you divide <m>47</m>
                        by <m>2</m>. But congruence doesn't always have to be the remainder.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>x</m> such that
                        <m>x \equiv 1 \pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We want <m>5\mid (x-1)</m> or that
                        there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that
                        <m>x-1 = 5k</m>, which means <m>x = 5k+1</m>. In other
                        words, every such integer <m>x</m> is <m>5k+1</m> for some
                        <m>k\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.
                        We could write this in <q>roster notation</q> as 
                        <m>\{\dots, -4, 1, 6, 11, \dots \}</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>x</m> such that
                        <m>3x\equiv 6 \pmod{9}</m> (or say that none exist).
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We want <m>9\mid (3x-6)</m>, or that there exists an integer
                        <m>k</m> such that <m>9k = 3x-6</m>. That is, <m>3x = 9k-6</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>x</m> such that
                        <m>5x\equiv 3 \pmod{7}</m> (or say that none exist).
                    </p>
                </statement>
                 <solution>
                    <p>
                        We want <m>7\mid (5x-3)</m>, or <m></m> there exists 
                        an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>5x-3=7k</m>. 
                        This means we want an integer such that <m>5x = 3 + 7k.</m>
                        When <m>k=1</m>, we have <m>5x</m> we can choose <m>x=2</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
               
            </task>
        </activity>

        <activity workspace="1in">
            <title>For fun</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    These problems are for people who want an additional
                    challenge/more fun.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For which integers <m>n</m> does <m>6\mid n</m> and
                        <m>6\mid (n+15)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>6\mid n </m> and <m>6\mid (n+15)</m>. Then there 
                        exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>6k = n</m> and 
                        an integer <m>j</m> such that <m>6j = n+15</m>, or <m>n = 6j-15</m>.
                        This woudl mean <m>6k = 6j-15</m>, or <m>6k - 6j = 15</m>. Then
                        <m>6(k-j) = 15</m>, or <m> 6 \mid 15 </m> which is not true. 
                        So that's a contradiction and there is no such integer.
                        
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the remainder when <m>2^{100}</m> is divided by
                        7?
                        (Hint: find a pattern in powers of 2 mod 7.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If you know <m>a \mid b</m>, what are 3 other divides
                        expressions you could say?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        We saw in class, that if you take a type <m>1</m>
                        integer and multiply with another type <m>1</m> integer
                        the result is a type <m>1</m> integer.
                        This is equivalent to saying if
                        <m>x\equiv 1 \pmod{3}\</m> and
                        <m>y\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>, then
                        <m>xy\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
                        So if we are multiplying <q>mod 3</q>, then
                        <m>1 \cdot 1 = 1</m>.
                        What is <m>2\cdot 2</m> <q>mod 3</q>?
                        Can you fill out a multiplication table for 3?
                        What about 4?
                        Do you see any patterns?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>
</handout>